\section{Find an object, then find a definition}

The loader's file search answers which object supplies each dependency name. Symbol search answers which eligible definition satisfies a reference from one of the mapped objects. These are separate operations. Seeing a library in a dependency list does not mean that every unqualified symbol in that library is bound to it. Levine explains the historical ELF model as a chain of per-object symbol tables rather than a freshly merged run-time table \cite[chapter~10]{levine2000linkers}. The tables can be searched efficiently with per-object hashes. A hash only finds candidates within an object; it cannot say which candidate wins between objects.

At runtime the dynamic linker obtains symbol data from \texttt{PT\_DYNAMIC} and its tags. \texttt{DT\_SYMTAB} and \texttt{DT\_STRTAB} denote the dynamic symbol and string tables, commonly represented as sections named \texttt{.dynsym} and \texttt{.dynstr}. The ordinary \texttt{.symtab} can contain private or diagnostic information and may be absent without affecting dynamic lookup. Current GNU/Linux objects can use a GNU hash table as well as or instead of the classic System V hash table in Levine's account. The lookup key can also include a symbol version. A name match is insufficient when the reference requires a particular version.

\section{Scope belongs to a reference}

A search scope is an ordered set of objects considered for a given operation. For a simple glibc startup with no special policies, the base global scope begins with the main program and lists dependencies in breadth-first order. They are visited in the order of the dependency lists at each depth, with duplicates suppressed. Not every operation searches this same list. A normal \texttt{dlsym} call with a handle returned by \texttt{dlopen} searches the named object and its dependency tree breadth-first. A newly loaded local group need not contribute its definitions to the global search list used by unrelated objects. The term \texttt{RTLD\_LOCAL} concerns visibility to other objects, not isolation or a restriction on what code in that group can do.

Under normal lookup rules, a default-visible definition in a shared library may be preempted, even for an internal use of its public name. Hidden symbols, in contrast, do not supply definitions to other objects and references to a library's own hidden definitions need no external search. Protected visibility permits an export while requiring references inside the defining object to bind there. These are reasons to build a small public interface; no amount of hash acceleration compensates for unintentionally publishing an internal helper's name.

