\section{Trace an ordered lookup and a failed match}

Model ordinary startup resolution for a graph in which the main image needs A then B and A needs C. Assume all candidates in the table are ordinary global definitions, any unrelated system libraries provide none of these names, and this reference requires the version \texttt{METER\_1}.

\begin{verbatim}
for obj in [main, A, B, C]:       # breadth-first, not recursive DFS
    for def in hash_candidates(obj, "meter_read"):
        if def.name != "meter_read": continue
        if not defined_visible_version_1(def): continue
        return obj.bias + def.value  # use the provider's bias
report unresolved required symbol
\end{verbatim}

Let A export \texttt{meter\_read} only at \texttt{METER\_2}. Let B and C each export \texttt{meter\_read} at \texttt{METER\_1}. With B's bias \texttt{0x71000000} and linked symbol value \texttt{0x1230}, the startup lookup returns \texttt{0x71001230}. A depth-first traversal would reach C before B and get the wrong answer. C at bias \texttt{0x72000000} with value \texttt{0x1480} would return \texttt{0x72001480} instead. Test the invariant that a lookup returns the earliest eligible definition in \emph{its own} scope: removing B's eligible export causes selection of C, and removing C's as well causes a required lookup to fail. This deliberately simple fixture does not model weak undefined references or GNU-specific symbol types; in particular, an unresolved weak reference can instead have a zero symbol value.

