In a local GNU ELF test, keep the \texttt{METER\_1} version node present in B while removing its \texttt{meter\_read} export. If the executable recorded a version requirement from B, removing the node could trigger a dependency version error before name lookup runs. Keeping a separate export in that node isolates the lookup test.

\section{Binding time changes when failure is observed}

Levine traces a classic i386 PLT path in which an unresolved jump slot calls the linker with enough information to identify the referencing image and relocation. The x86-64 ABI supplement gives its own sequence, with RIP-relative slot access. Conceptually, the resolver consults the proper scope, updates the slot, and transfers control to the resolved target. A subsequent call can use the slot without repeating the search. The selection rules and the time of selection are different concerns. Eager binding, requested for example through the GNU linker's \texttt{-z now}, resolves applicable PLT slots before handing control to the program; a deferred unresolved call can instead fail at first use. Do not infer that all dynamic references can be lazy: ordinary data addresses must already be available to the code that reads them.

When a locally built library returns an unexpected result, first verify the mapped dependency identity, then inspect its exported name, binding, visibility, and version with \texttt{readelf --dyn-syms -W} and \texttt{readelf -VW}. Compare the search you expected with the reference's actual scope. A missing definition, an out-of-scope definition, and a version mismatch can all defeat lookup, but they imply different fixes.
